"Artists call the system linear perspective. Its first rule is about parallel lines. Lines that run away from you, side by side, look as if they meet at a single point. That point is called the vanishing point. The sides of a road and the rails of a railway are the examples everyone knows. In a hallway the edges of the floor and the ceiling do the same thing, and so do the tops of the doors.": "9ac3e8ccc34d",
"Linear perspective is one way of drawing space. It is not the only way. It assumes one eye that never moves, while you have two eyes that are always moving. Chinese landscape painters chose a different path. Their pictures do not keep to a single fixed viewpoint, and the eye travels through them as if on a walk. Lesson 15 goes there. For now, find the eye level, find the vanishing point, and aim your lines at it.": "9c1d483b9907",
"Look at a photograph of a straight railway track, or stand at one end of a long hallway at school. The two sides are the same distance apart all the way along. You know that. Yet they look as if they are closing in on each other, and far away they seem to touch. Your paper is flat, and a room is not. The trick of drawing a room is to copy this closing-in exactly.": "4088323f1512",
"Look at a photograph of a straight railway track, or stand at one end of a long hallway at school. The two sides are the same distance apart all the way along. You know that. Yet they look as if they are closing in on each other, and far away they seem to touch. Your paper is flat, and a room is not. The trick of drawing a room is to copy this closing-in exactly. Artists call the system linear perspective. Its first rule is about parallel lines. Lines that run away from you, side by side, look as if they meet at a single point. That point is called the vanishing point. The sides of a road and the rails of a railway are the examples everyone knows. In a hallway the edges of the floor and the ceiling do the same thing, and so do the tops of the doors. Where is the vanishing point? It is on a level line at the height of your own eyes. Artists call it the eye-level line or the horizon line. Crouch down and it drops. Climb on a chair and it rises with you. Things below your eye level, like the floor, have lines that slope up toward it. Things above your eye level, like the ceiling, have lines that slope down. Step to the left and the vanishing point moves left as well. It is always straight in front of you. The second rule is about size. Things look smaller the farther away they are. Move a post twice as far from your eye and it looks half as tall. Move it three times as far and it looks one third as tall. The post has not changed. It simply takes up a smaller angle in your view. Perspective lets a painter work out exactly how big to draw each thing for its distance. People in Florence, Italy, worked this system out about six hundred years ago. It is generally accepted that the architect Filippo Brunelleschi made a series of experiments between 1415 and 1420, drawing the city's buildings in correct perspective. His painted panels are lost. In 1435 Leon Battista Alberti wrote the method down in a book called On Painting. A few years earlier the painter Masaccio had already used it for a wall painting of the Trinity in a church in Florence. Linear perspective is one way of drawing space. It is not the only way. It assumes one eye that never moves, while you have two eyes that are always moving. Chinese landscape painters chose a different path. Their pictures do not keep to a single fixed viewpoint, and the eye travels through them as if on a walk. Lesson 15 goes there. For now, find the eye level, find the vanishing point, and aim your lines at it.": "c6f0b162af36",
